    <!-- About -->
    <div class="section">
      <h2>About</h2>
      <p>I'm an instructional designer and learning systems strategist whose career has moved across technology, adult education, K-12, and nonprofit organizations, not because of circumstance, but because the work of designing learning that actually changes behavior travels well across contexts. Each move asked the same underlying question in a different setting: what does this specific group of people need to be able to do, and what's standing in the way?</p>
      <p>I've taught music in an underserved suburb of Cincinnati, where the constraint was resources and trust, not ability. I've led a congregation through a pandemic, where the constraint was fear and isolation, not willingness. I've contributed to the development of Grok at xAI, where the constraint was scale: getting a model to behave consistently for millions of people it will never meet individually. And I've built AI-supported learning systems for clinical education and competitive swimming, where the constraint was precision. The cost of a mistake there is a patient or a swimmer, not a support ticket.</p>
      <p>The through-line isn't the industry. It's the discipline of treating each of those constraints as real, on its own terms, rather than reaching for the same playbook everywhere.</p>
      <p>For the past 15 years, I've designed learning systems that improve performance, strengthen communication, and help people apply what they've learned.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Approach -->
    <div class="section">
      <h2>How I Approach the Work</h2>
      <p>I start by understanding the conditions around the work: the people involved, how communication actually happens (not how the org chart says it happens), the constraints teams are operating under, and what's getting in the way of performance. That means listening before designing, asking questions before proposing solutions, and staying curious about what isn't being said as much as what is. Learning design that ignores those conditions rarely sticks. It looks good in a slide deck and dies on contact with a real team.</p>
      <p>I see AI as a practical tool that supports human judgment, creativity, and decision-making rather than replacing it. In practice, that means every AI-integrated learning experience I build keeps a human in the loop at the point where a mistake would actually cost something: an instructor reviewing what the system recommends, a clear path for a learner to flag when the tool gets it wrong, and success measured by whether people got better at the job, not by whether the tool got used. Not automation for its own sake, but tools that make people better at what they already do, with the guardrails to know when they aren't.</p>
      <p>I'm most effective working closely with subject matter experts, stakeholders, and cross-functional teams, translating between what people know and what learners need. That translation work requires patience, systems thinking, and the ability to hold complexity without oversimplifying it, including the times when what the subject matter expert wants and what the learner actually needs aren't the same thing, and the design has to hold both rather than quietly picking one.</p>
    </div>

